sections.body:
A foreign key column can only point at *one* row on the other side, so it can express 'many posts belong to one author' but not 'many posts each have many tags, and many tags each apply to many posts.' A **many-to-many** relationship needs a third table -- a junction table, join table, or associative table -- sitting between the two, holding one row per *pairing*, with a foreign key pointing at each side.

```sql
CREATE TABLE tags (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE
);

CREATE TABLE post_tags (
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES tags(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    PRIMARY KEY (post_id, tag_id)
);
```

The composite primary key `(post_id, tag_id)` does two jobs at once: it gives the junction table the primary key every table needs, and -- because a primary key is also a uniqueness constraint -- it makes it impossible to insert the same `(post_id, tag_id)` pair twice, which is exactly the business rule 'a post can't have the same tag applied to it more than once.' `ON DELETE CASCADE` on both foreign keys is a genuinely good fit here, unlike the author/posts example: a junction row has no independent meaning of its own once either side of the pairing disappears, so cleaning it up automatically is correct rather than dangerous. Finding every tag on a post, or every post carrying a given tag, means joining through this table -- the subject of the next concept.

examples.code:
CREATE TABLE authors (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE posts (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title TEXT NOT NULL,
    author_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES authors(id)
);

INSERT INTO authors (name) VALUES ('Amara Chen');
-- authors.id = 1

INSERT INTO posts (title, author_id) VALUES ('Intro to SQL', 1);   -- succeeds
INSERT INTO posts (title, author_id) VALUES ('Ghost Post', 999);   -- fails

-- ERROR:  insert or update on table "posts" violates foreign key constraint
-- DETAIL:  Key (author_id)=(999) is not present in table "authors".

CREATE TABLE tags (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE
);

CREATE TABLE post_tags (
    post_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES tags(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    PRIMARY KEY (post_id, tag_id)
);

INSERT INTO tags (name) VALUES ('sql'), ('databases');
-- tags.id = 1 ('sql'), 2 ('databases')

INSERT INTO post_tags (post_id, tag_id) VALUES (1, 1);   -- succeeds: post 1 tagged 'sql'
INSERT INTO post_tags (post_id, tag_id) VALUES (1, 1);   -- fails: duplicate pair

-- ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint "post_tags_pkey"
-- DETAIL:  Key (post_id, tag_id)=(1, 1) already exists.

-- Unnormalized: customer data repeated on every order row
CREATE TABLE orders_unnormalized (
    order_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_email TEXT NOT NULL,
    customer_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    product_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    quantity INT NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO orders_unnormalized VALUES
    (1, 'amara@example.com', 'Amara Chen', 'Keyboard', 1),
    (2, 'amara@example.com', 'Amara Chen', 'Monitor', 2);

-- Amara changes her display name. Whoever writes this UPDATE has to
-- remember every row that duplicated it.
UPDATE orders_unnormalized SET customer_name = 'A. Chen' WHERE order_id = 1;
-- order_id = 2 now silently disagrees about her name -- an update anomaly.

-- Normalized: the fact "Amara's name is X" lives in exactly one row
CREATE TABLE customers (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    name TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE orders (
    order_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(id),
    product_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    quantity INT NOT NULL
);

UPDATE customers SET name = 'A. Chen' WHERE email = 'amara@example.com';
-- every order for this customer now reflects the new name, because
-- no order row stores the name at all -- only a reference to it.

exercises.solutionCode:
CREATE TABLE students (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE courses (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE enrollments (
    student_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES students(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    course_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES courses(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,
    grade TEXT,
    PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)
);

CREATE TABLE warehouses (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT NOT NULL,
    address TEXT NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE shipments (
    id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    warehouse_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES warehouses(id),
    order_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
    shipped_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

